Below about 1 eV, or 380,000 years after the Big Bang, the temperature became low enough for neutral hydrogen atoms to form through the reaction $e ^ { - } + p ^ { + } \mathrm { H } + \gamma .$ . This is the moment of recombination. At this point the density of free electrons dropped dramatically. Before recombination the strongest coupling between the photons and the rest of the plasma was through Thomson scattering, $e ^ { - } + \gamma e ^ { - } + \gamma$ . The sharp drop in the free electron density after recombination means that this process became ineficient and the photons decoupled. After decoupling the photons streamed freely through the universe and are observed today as the CMB.

